"""Builds pilots/L11/job.json — Columbia: the engineers who asked to look (long-form, ~7-8 min).
Sources: Columbia Accident Investigation Board (CAIB) Report, Vol. I (2003); NASA APPEL Columbia case study; UPI (2003) on the engineers' emails; Wikipedia 'Space Shuttle Columbia disaster' + citations."""
import json, pathlib, re
CH = [
 ("Sixteen minutes", """[serious] On the morning of February 1st, 2003, families waited at the Kennedy Space Center in Florida for the Space Shuttle Columbia to come home. Sixteen minutes before it was due to land, over Texas, it broke apart. All seven astronauts were killed. Seventeen years after Challenger, NASA had lost another shuttle. But this time, the danger had been spotted on the very first day of the mission. Engineers had watched it happen on video. They had asked, again and again, for a way to see the damage. And they had been told no. By the end of this file, you'll see the request that was cancelled, the email that was never sent, and the test that finally showed what had been hiding on Columbia's wing."""),
 ("Eighty-two seconds", """Columbia launched on January 16th, 2003, on a sixteen-day science mission called S T S one oh seven. Its crew of seven included Rick Husband, the commander, and Ilan Ramon, the first astronaut from Israel. About eighty-two seconds after liftoff, as the shuttle accelerated through the upper atmosphere, a chunk of insulating foam broke off the external fuel tank. It was about the size of a briefcase, and it weighed less than two pounds. But it hit the leading edge of Columbia's left wing at around five hundred miles an hour. Nobody saw it live. It was found the next day, when engineers reviewed the launch films. The leading edge of the wing was made of panels of reinforced carbon. They had to survive temperatures of nearly three thousand degrees during reentry. If one was broken, superheated gas could get inside the wing. And foam had been hitting shuttles for years. Pieces had come off on many earlier flights, including one just three months before. Each time, the shuttle had come home. Over time, NASA had come to treat foam strikes as a maintenance problem, not a safety one."""),
 ("Asking to look", """A team of engineers was assembled to assess the damage. One of its leaders was Rodney Rocha, a NASA structural engineer. They had a problem. The computer model they used to predict damage had been built for tiny pieces of debris, and this piece was hundreds of times larger than anything it had been tested against. They didn't know how bad the damage was. And they wanted to find out. Over the next few days, engineers made at least three separate requests for images of Columbia in orbit. The U.S. military had ground telescopes and other assets that might be able to photograph the wing. One request had actually been started. But on January 22nd, the chair of the Mission Management Team, Linda Ham, had it cancelled. The reasoning was that nobody had identified a requirement for the images. To the engineers, that was backwards. They couldn't prove there was a requirement without the pictures. Rocha wrote an email in response. In his humble technical opinion, he wrote, this was the wrong answer, and bordering on irresponsible. He showed it to colleagues. He never sent it to management."""),
 ("No concern", """On day eight of the mission, a flight director emailed Husband and the pilot, Willie McCool, to tell them about the foam strike. He wrote that there was no concern for damage to the wing, and that it was not even worth mentioning, except that reporters might ask. The engineers' team had concluded that the wing would probably survive, though they had no way to confirm it. At a meeting on January 24th, a short briefing on the foam was presented to the Mission Management Team. According to the investigation board, it was accepted with little discussion. The board later noted that no one at that meeting asked what would happen if the analysis was wrong. Meanwhile, Columbia's crew completed their science work in orbit, around the clock, for more than two weeks. They had no idea that a hole in their wing might be waiting for them on the way home."""),
 ("February 1st", """On the morning of February 1st, Columbia fired its engines and began the long fall back to Earth. As it plunged into the atmosphere, superheated air began to flow into its left wing through the damaged panel. At first, it showed up only as strange sensor readings. Temperatures in the wing rose. Then sensors began to go offline, one after another. In Mission Control, a flight controller reported the loss of tire pressure readings on the left side. The flight director, LeRoy Cain, asked for more information. Commander Husband started to reply. Roger, uh. And then the signal was lost. Just before nine a.m. Eastern time, about two hundred thousand feet above Texas, at more than eighteen times the speed of sound, Columbia broke apart. People on the ground saw streaks of light across the sky. Debris rained down across Texas and Louisiana. Over the following months, searchers recovered more than eighty thousand pieces. Rick Husband. Willie McCool. Michael Anderson. Kalpana Chawla. David Brown. Laurel Clark. Ilan Ramon."""),
 ("What the file tells us", """An independent board, the Columbia Accident Investigation Board, was set up to find out what happened. In July 2003, its investigators did something simple. They fired a piece of foam, about the same size, at about the same speed, at a real wing panel. It blew a hole about sixteen inches across. People in the room reportedly gasped. The board found that if the damage had been spotted early, a rescue mission using another shuttle, Atlantis, might have been possible. It would have been difficult and risky, but possible. And it found something else. The board said NASA's culture had played as large a role as the foam itself. The same pattern that had doomed Challenger had come back. Warning signs had become normal. People who raised concerns couldn't prove the danger, so the danger was assumed away. So here is what the file tells us. Columbia wasn't lost because nobody noticed. It was lost because the people who noticed were asked to prove there was a problem, without being allowed to look. If you had been in Rodney Rocha's place, would you have sent that email? Tell me in the comments. This is Dossier Decoded."""),
]
job = {"slug": "L11-columbia", "pillar": "Turned out true", "format": "long-form",
 "script": " ".join(t for _, t in CH), "chapters": [{"title": t, "text": x} for t, x in CH],
 "pdf": None, "commons": [
  {"slug": "cockpit", "query": "STS-107 crew in orbit Spacehab", "n": 1},
  {"slug": "husband", "query": "Rick Husband astronaut portrait", "n": 1},
  {"slug": "ramon", "query": "Ilan Ramon astronaut portrait", "n": 1},
  {"slug": "chawla", "query": "Kalpana Chawla portrait", "n": 1},
  {"slug": "mcc", "query": "Mission Control Houston STS-107 February 1 2003", "n": 1},
  {"slug": "hangar", "query": "Columbia debris reconstruction hangar Kennedy", "n": 1},
  {"slug": "rcc", "query": "Space Shuttle wing leading edge reinforced carbon-carbon panels", "n": 1},
  {"slug": "atlantis", "query": "Space Shuttle Atlantis on launch pad", "n": 1},
  {"slug": "return", "query": "STS-114 Discovery launch 2005", "n": 1}],
 "sfx": [{"slug": "drone", "text": "dark low ambient drone, tense documentary underscore, slow pulsing sub bass, no melody, seamless", "seconds": 22},
  {"slug": "stamp", "text": "a heavy rubber stamp slamming down on paper on a wooden desk, single hit, close up", "seconds": 1.2},
  {"slug": "paper", "text": "a single sheet of paper being slid across a desk and flipped, close up", "seconds": 1.2},
  {"slug": "hit", "text": "deep cinematic impact hit, single boom, short tail", "seconds": 1.5},
  {"slug": "rumble", "text": "rocket launch rumble, deep roar building", "seconds": 3},
  {"slug": "radio", "text": "NASA mission control radio static and quindar beep", "seconds": 2},
  {"slug": "boom", "text": "distant double sonic boom rolling across the sky", "seconds": 3}]}
p = pathlib.Path(__file__).parent
(p / "job.json").write_text(json.dumps(job, indent=1, ensure_ascii=False))
w = len(re.sub(r"\[.*?\]", "", job["script"]).split())
print("words", w, "est min", round(w / 2.75 / 60, 1), [len(x) for _, x in CH])
